<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ============================================================================
  RM_GreySeaTerrains.xml              TerminalBiomes · authored 2026-09-26
  ============================================================================
  The Grey Sea's own ground, above and below the waterline. Five terrains
  serving four filed items:

    RM_SaltCrustShore   GREYSEA_SHORE_MUTATOR_SPECIFICS_1  (surface)
    RM_BrineChannel     GREYSEA_FLOOR_FORMATIONS_1         (floor, Q3)
    RM_ChimneySeepFloor GREYSEA_FLOOR_FORMATIONS_1         (floor)
    RM_BrinePoolShallow GREYSEA_BRINE_POOL_DEFENCE_1       (floor)
    RM_BrinePoolDeep    GREYSEA_BRINE_POOL_DEFENCE_1       (floor)

  ⛔ NOT authored in src/RimMandrake/FlowWorks/Defs/LiquidTypes/TerrainDefs/
  RM_WaterBrine.xml, even though the pool grade is the saturated-PLUS step
  above RM_WaterBrineShallow/Deep. That file's own header says it is
  GENERATED by generate_liquid_suite.py from a LIQUID_ROWS table and must be
  regenerated, never hand-edited. Adding a row there is a FlowWorks change
  against a frozen dump and belongs to LIQUID_TYPES_MOD_1, not to this wave.
  These live in the biome's own mod and reference nothing of FlowWorks'.

  ✅ RE-CHECKED under LIQUID_TERRAIN_AUTHORED_TWICE_1 (closed 2026-09-27):
  RM_BrinePoolShallow/Deep and FlowWorks' RM_WaterBrineShallow/Deep share the
  English words "brine pool" but are NOT the same content forked twice —
  RM_WaterBrine* is RM_GreySea's own live shore/coastal water (RM_GreySea.xml,
  RM_Miasma.xml, RUT_Miasma.xml all reference it directly) while these two are
  a rarer, deliberately harsher floor grade with their own encasement hazard
  mechanism, not wired into any biome's default water fields at all. No
  numbers disagree because none are shared; nothing here was folded into the
  generator and nothing needed deleting.

  ✅ RE-CHECKED AGAIN under TERMINALBIOMES_LIQUID_RETARGET_1 (Q7 ownership
  ruling execution, 2026-09-28): same conclusion holds, and confirmed from
  the live wiring, not just the description text — RM_GreySea.xml's own
  <deepTerrain>/<shallowTerrain> (its default sea water) are ALREADY
  FlowWorks' RM_WaterBrineDeep/RM_WaterBrineShallow directly
  (MayRequire="mandrake.rm.flowworks"). RM_BrinePoolShallow/Deep here are a
  separate floor hazard grade painted only by GenStep_GreySeaFloorDressing
  (DivingInteraction), never the sea's default water. There is nothing
  generic left to retarget for brine: this pair is a keeper, not a
  duplicate. Do not re-open this a third time on a defName-similarity read
  alone.
  ============================================================================
  THE SHORE: "every Grey Sea shoreline carries the crusty whiteness"
  ============================================================================
  Owner, 2026-09-26, with reference art:
  design/Jawa/worldbuilding/reference/grey_sea/02_crusted_white_shoreline.png
  — lumpy, cauliflower-textured white salt GROWN OVER red-brown stones at a
  flat waterline. "Every" is a coverage rule: no Grey Sea shore tile without
  it.

  🔴 It is NOT RUT_Jawa_SaltCrust. That def
  (src/RimUtinni/UtinniPatches/Defs/TerrainDefs/JawaSaltCrust.xml) is a FLAT
  EVAPORITE PAN by its own description — the right family, the wrong texture
  for this image, and Utinni-tier besides. The crust here is a knobbly
  growth on stone.

  RULED 2026-09-26 (Q4): the shore is the white crust TERRAIN **plus** salt
  domes scattered on it, and **no harvestable shore salt** — the sea-floor
  salt stays the prize. So this def yields nothing and carries no resource.

  ============================================================================
  THE POOL GRADE, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT DO YET
  ============================================================================
  ⚠️ RM_BrinePoolShallow/Deep are the saturated-plus grade the sheet asks for
  and they are INERT today: the crystallisation defence — touch a pool and
  you are encased as an object that must be mined out (RULED 2026-09-26,
  Q1(b)) — is C# and is GREYSEA_BRINE_POOL_DEFENCE_1. These defs are what
  that mechanism will trigger ON. ⛔ Do not read "the terrain exists" as
  "the pool is deadly"; today walking into one is merely slow.

  The `RM_GreyBrinePool` tag is the hook the contact trigger will read, so it
  is on both and on nothing else.
  ============================================================================
-->
<Defs>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       SURFACE — the crusted white shoreline. Carried onto every Grey
       shore by RM_SeaShoreExtension's beachTerrain field, which
       RM_TileMutatorWorker_SeaCoast already reads per-sea (measured
       2026-09-26: the route exists, only the data was missing).
       ================================================================== -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>RM_SaltCrustShore</defName>
    <label>salt crust</label>
    <description>Salt grown over the stones at the waterline in knobbly white heads, layer on popcorn layer, until the beach underneath is only a shape you can still make out. It crunches, it holds a footprint, and it comes back within a season of being broken. Every shore of this sea has it, and the band of it gets wider every year the sea gets smaller.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/RoughStone</texturePath>
    <color>(238,236,228)</color>
    <isPaintable>false</isPaintable>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <renderPrecedence>355</renderPrecedence>
    <pathCost>3</pathCost>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>2</Beauty>
      <Cleanliness>-1</Cleanliness>
    </statBases>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
      <li>Medium</li>
      <li>Heavy</li>
    </affordances>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <takeFootprints>true</takeFootprints>
    <tags>
      <li>RM_GreySaltCrust</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       FLOOR — the brine channel. RULED 2026-09-26 (Q3): the kelp spec's
       "brine rivers" are a CHANNEL TERRAIN STRIP running from the
       chimney field downhill into a pool. No vanilla river machinery —
       the pocket map has no river genstep and the surface sea is
       allowRivers false.

       It gives RM_GlassVeilKelp its only home (wildTerrainTags
       RM_GreyBrineChannel) and gives the floor a legible gradient
       toward the danger: follow the channel down and you arrive at the
       pool that kills you.

       ⚠️ NOT PAINTED YET. The genstep that carves it is the one owed
       C# of this wave. The def is real; the strip is not on any map.
       ================================================================== -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>RM_BrineChannel</defName>
    <label>brine channel</label>
    <description>A shallow groove in the sea floor carrying brine heavier than the sea it lies in — a river inside the water, running downhill to the pools with a visible surface of its own. Walking it is like wading a second, denser sea. Everything worth avoiding down here is somewhere at the bottom of one of these.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/Sand</texturePath>
    <color>(198,214,210)</color>
    <isPaintable>false</isPaintable>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <renderPrecedence>360</renderPrecedence>
    <pathCost>18</pathCost>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>1</Beauty>
      <Cleanliness>-2</Cleanliness>
    </statBases>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
      <li>Medium</li>
      <li>Heavy</li>
    </affordances>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <takeFootprints>false</takeFootprints>
    <tags>
      <li>RM_GreyBrineChannel</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       FLOOR — the chimney seep apron. The ring of warm, mineral-caked
       floor around an RM_SaltChimney, and the ONLY place
       RM_SaltChimneyVine may grow ("these grow only around warm mineral
       seeps or chemical vents", his words).

       🔑 This apron is why the vine needs NO placement code: paint the
       apron, and the vanilla Plants genstep grows the vines on it by
       itself.

       ⚠️ NOT PAINTED YET — same owed genstep as the channel.
       ================================================================== -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="NaturalTerrainBase">
    <defName>RM_ChimneySeepFloor</defName>
    <label>seep apron</label>
    <description>The skirt of floor around a salt chimney, caked hard with everything the vent has dropped since it started: pale mineral over black vent rock, warm underfoot, and the only warm thing on this floor. Nothing grows anywhere near it except what has learned to live off what is coming out.</description>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/RoughStone</texturePath>
    <color>(202,196,182)</color>
    <isPaintable>false</isPaintable>
    <edgeType>FadeRough</edgeType>
    <renderPrecedence>358</renderPrecedence>
    <pathCost>4</pathCost>
    <statBases>
      <Beauty>1</Beauty>
      <Cleanliness>-1</Cleanliness>
    </statBases>
    <affordances>
      <li>Light</li>
      <li>Medium</li>
      <li>Heavy</li>
    </affordances>
    <fertility>0</fertility>
    <takeFootprints>false</takeFootprints>
    <tags>
      <li>RM_GreyChimneySeep</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <!-- ==================================================================
       FLOOR — the pool grade. The sheet's "lakes at the bottom of a
       sea": brine so dense it has its own surface and its own shore,
       and it is the deadliest thing in the biome.

       "Everything that enters dies; everything that dies there keeps"
       (sheet §3). Ban 1: no survivable brine-pool entry — harvest
       happens at their shores.

       ⚠️ INERT TODAY. See the file header: the encasement mechanism is
       GREYSEA_BRINE_POOL_DEFENCE_1 and is not built.
       ================================================================== -->
  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterShallowBase">
    <defName>RM_BrinePoolShallow</defName>
    <label>brine pool margin</label>
    <description>The shore of a pool: brine so concentrated it will not mix with the sea standing on top of it, lying still in its basin with a visible skin. This is as close as anyone gets on purpose. Everything valuable in the Grey Sea was dropped into one of these at some point, and everything that went in after it is still standing in there.</description>
    <renderPrecedence>396</renderPrecedence>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterShallowRamp</texturePath>
    <color>(214,222,214)</color>
    <waterBodyType>Saltwater</waterBodyType>
    <pathCost>60</pathCost>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>RM_GreyBrinePool</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

  <TerrainDef ParentName="WaterDeepBase">
    <defName>RM_BrinePoolDeep</defName>
    <label>brine pool</label>
    <description>Ultra-dense brine filling the deepest basin of the floor, so much heavier than the sea above it that the boundary between the two is a surface you can see and things float on. Nothing lives in it. Nothing rots in it. Whatever the pool has taken is still exactly where it fell, jacketed in salt, in the dark, waiting for someone to work out how to get it back.</description>
    <renderPrecedence>397</renderPrecedence>
    <texturePath>Terrain/Surfaces/WaterDeepRamp</texturePath>
    <color>(196,206,198)</color>
    <waterBodyType>Saltwater</waterBodyType>
    <tags>
      <li>Water</li>
      <li>RM_GreyBrinePool</li>
    </tags>
  </TerrainDef>

</Defs>
